      "target": "../sections/page-003.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "FOREWORD\n\nInformation, Education and Communication (IEC) is one of the most essential\ncomponents of an AIDS prevention programme. When planned well, within the context of an\noverall HIV/AIDS prevention and control programme, IEC can be a very effective intervention\nto bring about appropriate changes in behaviour, especially among populations with high-risk\nbehaviour.\n\nIn the South-East Asia Region of WHO, where the number of HIV infections is\nprojected to be as high as 10 million by the year 2000, unprotected heterosexual activity is\nestablished to be the predominant mode of transmission of HI",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The foreword describes information, education and communication as a key part of AIDS prevention, especially for populations with high-risk behaviour in South-East Asia. It notes that heterosexual transmission predominates in the Region and that the course of the epidemic depends on well-planned IEC that motivates responsible sexual behaviour.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-205344/blocks/prose-page-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 4",
      "pageStart": 4,
      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/page-004.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SEA/AIDS/81\n\n1. INTRODUCTION\n\nThe pandemic of Acquired Immuno-deficiency Syndrome (AIDS) poses a unique\nchallenge to public health planners and programme developers. With no cure in sight, we are\nforced to look closely at behavioural patterns and how to alter them to prevent AIDS. Since\nAIDS is primarily a sexually transmitted disease (STD), it is important that policy-makers,\ncommunicators and public health professionals examine the sexual behaviour patterns of the\ncommunities they are serving and develop strategies aimed at behavioural change.\n\nInformation, education and communication (IEC)",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the guide by arguing that, with no cure for AIDS, prevention relies on understanding and changing sexual behaviour. It begins to define IEC as a broad range of approaches, activities and outputs beyond printed materials.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-205344/blocks/prose-page-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 5",
      "pageStart": 5,
      "pageEnd": 5,
      "target": "../sections/page-005.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SEA/AIDS/81\nPage 2\n\nposters hanging on clinic walls, materials are only one component. Effective IEC makes use\nof a full range of approaches and activities.\n\nApproaches may range from the use of mass media to inform or establish positive norms\namong the general population to the use of targeted interpersonal communication to help those\nat particular risk evaluate their own behaviour and develop new personal skills. IEC activities\nmay include designing and providing training in communication skills, carrying out research\non audiences to determine what information is needed and the most effectiv",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that IEC spans mass media, interpersonal communication, training and audience research, and describes how the guide is meant to help programme managers. It then outlines the projected growth of HIV infections in Asia, expected to exceed those in sub-Saharan Africa by 2000.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-205344/blocks/prose-page-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 6",
      "pageStart": 6,
      "pageEnd": 6,
      "target": "../sections/page-006.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SEA/AIDS/81\nPage 3\n\nFigure 1. Estimated and projected cumulative adult HIV\ninfections in Asia and Africa\n\nMillions\n\n16\nEJ Asia\n\n14\nWi Africa\n\n12\n\n10\n\n8\n\n6\n\n| iT,\n\n2\naul ;\n1985 | 1990\n\nThe greatest tragedy, besides medical and health care costs, will be the loss of thousands\nof lives, particularly those of young adults in their most productive years and infants born to\nHIV-infected mothers, which will directly affect child survival rates.\n\n3. THE ROLE OF IEC IN HIV/AIDS PREVENTION\n\nAll countries in the Region now have national AIDS prevention and care programmes\nand are organizing efforts to co",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents a figure comparing projected cumulative adult HIV infections in Asia and Africa and notes the loss of young adult lives. It then opens the section on IEC's role, listing desired behaviour changes such as delayed sexual debut, fewer partners, condom use, STD treatment and clean syringes.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
