    {
      "id": "library/10665-205344/blocks/prose-page-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 11",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/page-011.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SEA/AIDS/81\nPage 8\n\nMoral messages may turn away the very people you want to reach or deny life-\nsaving information to those in need\n\nAIDS education necessitates talking about sexual behaviour and methods of protection\nfrom HIV through sexual intercourse. To date, the only known method, barring abstinence,\nis condom use. However, in many countries, governments and religious organizations have\nprohibited the promotion of condom use with the argument that it would promote promiscuity.\nThis has led to disastrous results in some countries. Studies have conclusively demonstrated\nthat education on s",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Argues that moralistic messages and bans on condom promotion can deny people life-saving information, citing evidence that sex education does not increase sexual activity. It recommends messages that are positive, practical and offer options for action.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-205344/blocks/prose-page-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 12",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/page-012.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SEA/AIDS/81\nPage 9\n\nyou inject drugs, so don’t inject: if you can’t avoid injecting, don’t share needles;\nif you can’t avoid sharing, at least clean the needles before sharing’. Such\nbehaviour options also apply to sexual transmission. For example: ‘Your chances\nof contracting HIV/STDs increase if you have multiple sexual partners; so abstain\nfrom sex or stick to one uninfected partner; or practise safer sex such as condom\nuse for every sexual encounter in situations of risk.’\n\n4.3 Targeting AIDS Education\n\nAnother important lesson learned is the need to prioritize programme activities. Not\nev",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives examples of graded risk-reduction messages for drug injection and sexual behaviour, then discusses prioritizing and targeting AIDS education at those most at risk. It also stresses that IEC must be part of an integrated package with condoms, STD treatment and counselling, delivered through multisectoral collaboration.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
