      "target": "../sections/page-019.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SEA/AIDS/81\nPage 16\n\nand interactive media must be created. In many cases, folk media such as puppetry, drama and\nstory telling, can be used quite effectively to support interpersonal communication and should\nbe actively considered as part of the overall IEC plan.\n\n5.3.3 Develop IEC materials\n\nDevelopment of draft materials is based on decisions about messages, media and\nchannels to be used for delivery to each target group. Materials may consist of radio/TV spots,\nbooklets, posters, handouts or hoardings, but these are not all. IEC also involves tools for use\nin interpersonal communication. I",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recommends combining passive and interactive media, including folk media like puppetry and drama, and describes developing draft IEC materials for different audiences. It stresses pretesting materials, especially for non-literate audiences, and summarizes the design, pretest and revise cycle.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
