      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/page-022.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SEA/AIDS/81\nPage 19\n\nwomen at antenatal clinics on the risks of HIV infection and pregnancy’. Output refers to the\nquantity of items used to carry out activities or to the quantified result of carrying out a task.\nExamples are numbers of brochures printed or distributed, numbers of personnel trained, and\nnumbers of condoms distributed.\n\n(b) Adoption of monitoring methods, and development and\nadoption of appropriate tools for monitoring\n\nProgramme activities can be monitored through checklists of observations to be made\nduring supervisory visits, regular checking of workplans, and use of report",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how outputs of an HIV/AIDS information, education and communication (IEC) programme are counted and outlines monitoring methods such as supervisory checklists, workplan reviews and routine reports. It also stresses quick feedback on IEC materials, covering audience interest, comprehension, format and visual appeal.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
