      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 23",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 23,
      "target": "../sections/page-023.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SEA/AIDS/81\nPage 20\n\n5.4.2 Evaluation\n\nEvaluation is the process of collecting and analysing information at regular intervals\nabout the effectiveness and impact of either particular parts of the programme or the\nprogramme as a whole. A variety of different evaluation methods is possible depending on\nprogramme needs. Regardless of method, planning for evaluation, including development of\nprogramme indicators and planning for information collection, should take place at the\n\nbeginning of the programmes to ensure that essential data will be available when needed.\n\nImpact is measured against the p",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes evaluation of the IEC programme, noting that indicators and data collection must be planned from the outset and that impact is judged against objectives using baseline data. It lists impact-related questions evaluation can answer and says reviews should also cover training and service use.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
