    {
      "id": "library/10665-205344/blocks/prose-page-026",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 26",
      "pageStart": 26,
      "pageEnd": 26,
      "target": "../sections/page-026.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SEA/AIDS/81\nPage 23\n\nAnnex 1\n\nIEC FOR SPECIFIC TARGET AUDIENCES —- YOUTH AS AN EXAMPLE\n\nThis section illustrates the use of the IEC planning and implementation process with one\ntarget group: young persons - adolescents and youth - from 10 to 24 years of age. This group\nwill need to be divided into segments due to the wide range of developmental stages and\ncultural norms.\n\n1 PLANNING\n1.1 Situational Analysis\n(1) ‘National policy and organizational structure\n\nIn the case of youth it is important to know what policies are in place for: the health\nof youth of each segment, including children in sc",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Opens Annex 1, which applies the IEC planning process to young people aged 10 to 24, starting with situational analysis of national youth policy and the approach to IEC for youth. It notes that the data gathered will help segment the group, design messages and involve youth in developing materials.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-205344/blocks/prose-page-027",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 27",
      "pageStart": 27,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/page-027.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SEA/AIDS/81\nPage 24\n\nIdentifying and using peers as communicators;\n\nIdentifying suitable media and means of reaching the target group (including\nthrough influencers);\n\nDevelopment of appropriate school curricula on AIDS; and\n\nDevelopment of co-curricular activities for AIDS.\n\n1.2 Target Group Identification\nYouth, both male and female, can be further segmented in most countries as follows:\n— Youth in school (urban and rural);\n— Youth out of school in the urban setting;\n\n— Youth out of school in the rural setting;\n\nStreet youth or street children; and\n\nUniversity students.\n\nEach group has diffe",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues the youth annex with planning activities such as peer communicators and school curricula, then segments youth into in-school, out-of-school urban and rural, street and university groups. It notes the importance of parents and teachers and begins listing desired behavioural goals such as delaying sexual initiation.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
